<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaiya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chaiya is a town and district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat-thani">Surat Thani</a> province in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bandon">Bay of Bandon</a>. An important centre in the era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, it is known for the temple of Wat Phra Borommathat Chaiya and for Buddhist sculptures and inscriptions, among them an inscription of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrabhanu">Chandrabhanu</a> of Tambralinga dated 1230.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaiya.jpg" alt="Chaiya" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KOSIN SUKHUM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Phra_Boromathat_Chaiya_(Thailand).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chaiya district lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>, with Cape Sui marking the northern end of the Bandon Bay, and borders districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat-thani">Surat Thani</a>, Ranong and Chumphon provinces. The east of the district is mostly flat, low coastal land, while to the west rise the mountains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phuket">Phuket</a> range, including Kaeng Krung National Park. The name may come from the Malay cahaya, meaning light or gleam, and some scholars derive it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Laem Pho Beach in the district is thought to have been a seaport of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> kingdom from the 7th to the 13th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> ceramics, pottery from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and glassware from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> have been found there. Similar finds at Ban Thung Tuek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takua-pa">Takua Pa</a> district, 200 km away on the other side of the isthmus, suggest an overland route between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a> and the Andaman coast that let traders avoid piracy in the narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>.</p><p>Wat Phra Borommathat is centred on a reconstructed stupa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> style, and two more former stupas nearby are now brick mounds. Inscription 23 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damrong-rajanubhab">Prince Damrong</a>'s Collected Inscriptions of Siam, now attributed to Wat Hua Wiang in Chaiya, is dated 697 of the Mahasakkarat era, that is 775 CE; on a stone shaped like a bai sema, it tells of the king of Srivijaya erecting three stupas there, and possibly the one at Wat Phra Borom That.</p><p>Another important temple near Chaiya is the forest temple Wat Suan Mohkha Phalaram, known by the short name Suan Mok or as Wat Than Nam Lai, the Monastery of Flowing Water. It was founded in 1932 by Phra Buddhadasa (1906–1993), a revered Buddhist teacher, and in 1959 it was relocated to its present site of 380 rai (150 acres).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chaiya is a former capital district (amphoe mueang) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat-thani">Surat Thani</a> province, and its main town is Talat Chaiya. A branch of the National Museum near Wat Phra Borommathat displays relics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> period. The district lies on the southern railway line and on Asian highway AH2, and is divided into nine sub-districts and 54 villages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chaiya district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Avalokiteshvaras of Bingin Jungut in Sumatra, Bidor in Perak and <b>Chaiya</b> in southern Thailand show Central Javanese influence long mislabelled Srivijayan, and the Bakong in Cambodia so resembles Borobudur that travellers or missions must have carried its design to the Khmer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its most famous ruler, Chandrabhanu Sridhamaraja, recorded in an inscription at <b>Chaiya</b> dated 1230, built up a navy and twice invaded Sri Lanka, partly to obtain a sacred relic.</blockquote><a href="/state/tambralinga">Tambralinga</a></li></ul></section>
